1 NOTE {geni:about_me} On August 25 1770, Dom José I (1750-1777) issued an alvará declaring that Dom Estêvão Soares de Mello, the lord of the Casa dos Donatários de Mello, and his sister, Dona Teresa de Mello, had been deprived of the na
2 CONC turalidade of their family, whom, as the king justified, they had insulted so much with their behavior.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, the royal decree provides no more details about the indignities of the siblings of the House of Mello; nevertheless, some have become public thanks to other sources. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For example, we know that Dona Teresa once pretended to be pregnant by her fiancé in order to be released from the Encarnación convent, where she lived in seclusion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, she accused her brother Dom Estêvão of being a deserter from war.
2 CONT
2 CONT These actions made them unworthy of representing the memory of their glorious ancestors so, once they had been divested of their familys naturalidade, and being reputados por estranhos, the Mello siblings and their descendants we
2 CONC re divested of all the Crown property and Military Orders, that they held. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the genealogist Felgueiras, this stripping of the familys naturalidadewas tantamount to their being deprived of their status of being natives of the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of the historians temptation to tell lineal stories and draw clear genealogies with lines which reach our days, the example of the Mello siblings and the concepts involved in the deprivation of their naturalidade- meanin
2 CONC g nativeness and family belonging- prove that, at least in connection with nobility and despite what has been stated above, polysemy remained in Portugal until the end of the Old Regime. I
2 CONT
2 CONT n the history of naturalidade, in its slow elaboration, it is clear that the legal aspects were relevant; but as it is demonstrated by the dissolute Mello siblings that in order to reach an accurate understanding of the full meani
2 CONC ng of being natural and what it entailed to be deprived of it in that period, it is important to go on researching along the path of identities, imaginaries and conflictive practices in the kingdoms of early modern Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dom Estêvão de Mello was exiled to Angola but, in the reign of Dona Maria I, his familysnaturalidade was restored and returned to him. Gayo (1992 [1938]: 130).
2 CONT
2 CONT in, http://www.scielo.mec.pt/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S1645-64322012000200002#113
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT JABOATÃO, A. DE S. M. Catálogo genealógico das principais famílias: que procederam de Albuquerques, e Cavalcantes em Pernambuco, e Caramurus na Bahia, tiradas de memorias, manuscritos antigos e fidedignos, autorizados por alguns e
2 CONC scritores, e em especial o Theatro Genealógico de D. Livisco de Nazáo Zarco e Colona, aliás Manoel de Carvalho de Atahide, e acrescentado o mais moderno, e confirmado tudo, assim moderno, como antigo com assentos dos livros de bap
2 CONC tizados, cazamentos, e enterros, que se guardam na Camara Ecceziastica da Bahia. '''Revista do Instituto Histórico e Geográfico Brasileiro''', Rio de Janeiro, tomo LII, parte 1, 1889.
